articles.19:
The following conduct is prohibited within a protected area of the historic and cultural city of Dai County:
(1) constructing buildings or structures that damage the traditional layout or historic character;
(2) damaging or demolishing without authorization a historic building with conservation value;
(3) removing without authorization old gates, windows, plaques, screen walls, or other decorative components from historic buildings or structures;
(4) occupying or damaging ancient and notable trees, old wells, memorial arches, pavilions, steles, historic lanes, or roads;
(5) carrying out exterior repairs or decoration on historic buildings, traditional residences, shops, or similar properties, or changing a courtyard's layout or structure, without authorization;
(6) excavating, widening, straightening, or otherwise altering traditional streets or roads, or excavating underground space, without authorization;
(7) damaging, moving, or demolishing public facilities without authorization; or
(8) any other conduct prohibited by laws or regulations.

articles.9:
Protected assets of the historic and cultural city in this Municipality include:
(1) historic urban areas represented by the Ancient City of Chaoyang;
(2) historic towns and villages, including Shengli Town in Chaoyang County and Shiyi Village in Dongdatun Township, and traditional villages, including Jinling Temple Village in Beipiao City;
(3) historic districts, including the Chaoyang Pump Factory area and the residential area of the Mashan Electric Machinery Factory;
(4) historic areas, including Yebaishou Station in Jianping County, and historic buildings, including the dormitories of Chaoyang Agricultural College;
(5) the remains of the modern-era Jinzhou–Chengde Railway in Chaoyang;
(6) cultural relics protection units, including the Niuheliang Site, the Sanyan Longcheng Site, Chaoyang North Pagoda, and Youshun Temple, as well as immovable cultural relics that have not been assigned a protection grade;
(7) revolutionary memorial facilities and sites, including the Zhao Shangzhi Martyrs Cemetery and the former site where Nie Er visited and performed for the Anti-Japanese Volunteer Army;
(8) industrial heritage, including the Beipiao coal-mining industrial heritage cluster and the Guangming Refractory Materials Factory in Kazuo;
(9) intangible cultural heritage, including Lingyuan shadow puppetry and shadow-puppet carving, Jianping paper-cutting, traditional production techniques for Lingta liquor, and the traditional brewing techniques for Tacheng aged vinegar in Kazuo;
(10) place-name heritage represented by Chaoyang County and Beipiao City;
(11) ancient and notable trees, including the king cypress at Paoshou Village in Heiniuyingzi Township, Chaoyang County, and the fragrant snowbell tree at Houxidi Village, Qingfengling Town; and
(12) other protected matters prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.21:
A system of designated conservation responsibility shall apply to the historic and cultural city. The responsible entity shall be determined as follows:
(1) for a historic urban area, the people's government of the Municipality, county, or county-level city where it is located;
(2) for a historic town or village, traditional village, historic district, or historic area, the people's government of the county, county-level city, or district where it is located;
(3) for a historic building, its owner; if the owner cannot be located or ownership is unclear, its user; if neither the owner nor the user can be identified, the people's government of the county, county-level city, or district where it is located shall designate the responsible entity; and
(4) for any other protected asset, the responsible entity prescribed by laws and regulations or, if none is prescribed, the entity designated by the people's government of the county, county-level city, or district where the asset is located.

articles.22:
The entity responsible for a historic urban area, historic town or village, traditional village, historic district, or historic area shall:
(1) maintain the traditional layout, spatial scale, and historic character and preserve the integrity of dependent natural landscapes and environmental features;
(2) improve infrastructure, public-service facilities, and the living environment;
(3) keep the protected area clean and orderly;
(4) identify and present its historical, cultural, and landscape value;
(5) take effective measures for fire prevention, theft prevention, disaster prevention, and mitigation;
(6) organize routine inspections, promptly stop conduct that endangers historical and cultural heritage, and notify the relevant departments; and
(7) perform other conservation responsibilities prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.23:
The person responsible for a historic building shall:
(1) carry out routine maintenance and repair;
(2) preserve its original height, scale, exterior appearance, and colors;
(3) ensure structural safety, assist the relevant departments in keeping fire-safety and disaster-prevention facilities and equipment operational, promptly address hazards when discovered, and report them to the relevant township people's government, subdistrict office, or administrative department; and
(4) perform other conservation responsibilities prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.29:
The following activities are prohibited within a protected area of a historic and cultural city, historic town, or historic village:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage the traditional layout or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, rivers, lakes, or roads designated for retention in the conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or similar facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, corrosive, or other hazardous substances;
(4) carving or defacing historic buildings; or
(5) other activities prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.30:
The following conduct in relation to historic buildings is strictly prohibited:
(1) altering, relocating, or demolishing one without authorization;
(2) damaging load-bearing structures or endangering building safety;
(3) carrying out repairs or maintenance, adding facilities, or changing the structure in a manner that damages its historic or artistic features or its spatial or architectural character;
(4) producing, storing, or selling explosive, flammable, toxic, radioactive, corrosive, or other hazardous substances inside one; or
(5) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.33:
Organizations and individuals are encouraged to participate lawfully in protecting and using historical and cultural resources by:
(1) establishing museums, exhibition halls, memorial halls, theme parks, science-education bases, and similar facilities;
(2) establishing studios or training centers for representative bearers of intangible cultural heritage to study and pass on that heritage;
(3) operating cultural inns or homestays;
(4) opening traditional workshops or shops;
(5) making, exhibiting, or promoting traditional handicrafts;
(6) establishing nonprofit organizations and carrying out volunteer services; or
(7) using other means of protection and use.

articles.13:
For the purposes of these Regulations, protection of the historic and cultural city of Huili means:
(1) protecting the city's historic character, traditional layout, historic buildings, and spatial scale;
(2) protecting its cultural relics and historic sites, streets and lanes with traditional character, historic buildings, traditional residences and courtyards, ancient and notable trees, old wells, and immovable cultural relics with historic and cultural character;
(3) protecting the natural landscape and environment of its scenic areas; and
(4) protecting the city's ethnic and folk traditions.

articles.33:
Resources of the historic and cultural city of Huili shall be developed and used reasonably. The following projects and activities are encouraged in its protection zone:
(1) research on the city's traditional culture;
(2) construction of folk-culture inns and other buildings consistent with the conservation plan;
(3) traditional handicraft workshops, folk crafts, and the production and sale of tourism products;
(4) research, development, and operation related to traditional food culture;
(5) traditional entertainment and folk-art performances;
(6) development, collection, exhibition, and trading of folk handicrafts; and
(7) development and operation of other products and cultural activities with local characteristics.

articles.11:
A conservation plan for a historic and cultural city shall meet the following requirements:
(1) preserve and continue the city's layout and distinctive character, protect cultural relics and historic sites, and carry on traditional culture;
(2) determine conservation principles and priorities in light of the nature, form, distribution, and spatial setting of historic and cultural remains, and protect and use cultural resources;
(3) provide integrated protection for commercial areas, handicraft districts, residential areas, and other historic areas with a traditional character;
(4) protect important revolutionary sites and carry on revolutionary traditions; and
(5) coordinate protection of the historic and cultural city with economic and social development and the cultural and ecological environment.

articles.20:
No organization or individual may engage in any of the following conduct that damages a historic and cultural city:
(1) damaging or demolishing buildings, structures, or other facilities designated for protection under a conservation plan;
(2) carrying out construction, blasting, sand excavation, soil extraction, or other activities that endanger the safety of cultural relics, historic sites, or revolutionary sites;
(3) changing topography or landforms in a manner that threatens protection of the city;
(4) occupying or damaging without authorization green spaces, river systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention under the conservation plan;
(5) erecting buildings or structures in violation of the rules in a key conservation area; or
(6) engaging in any other activity that damages protection of the historic and cultural city.

articles.3:
Protected assets and areas of the National Historic and Cultural City of Huai'an include:
(1) the historic urban area, bounded by Chuzhou Avenue to the east, the Jian River to the south, Wenqu, the southeastern shore of Xiaohu Lake, and the Inner Grand Canal to the west, and Xiangyu Avenue to the north;
(2) historic districts, including the Hexia Ancient Town Historic District, bounded by Guangji Bridge to the east, the Inner Grand Canal to the south, and natural streets and lanes to the west and north; and the Fuma Lane–Longwo Lane–Shangban Street Historic District, bounded by the western boundary of the Caoyun Square commercial street to the east, Zhenhuai Tower West Road and Ju Lane to the south, Fuma Lane, the Chuzhou Museum, and the Ju Lake Community Residents' Committee to the west, and Ximen Avenue to the north;
(3) historic areas, including the Old Ximen Avenue, Shuangdao Liu Lane, Taiqingguan Street, County East Street, Dongyue Temple, and Hebei Street–Guangming Street historic areas;
(4) immovable cultural relics, including the Former Residence of Zhou Enlai, the former site of the Central China Branch of the Communist Party of China Central Committee, the Huai'an Prefectural Office, the Qingliangang Site, and Wentong Pagoda, as well as underground cultural relics burial areas;
(5) historic buildings, ancient and notable trees, intangible cultural heritage, and historically continuous river and lake systems and roads; and
(6) other protected assets prescribed by laws and regulations.
Where other laws or regulations separately prescribe the protection of immovable cultural relics, underground cultural relics burial areas, ancient and notable trees, intangible cultural heritage, or other assets within the protected area of the National Historic and Cultural City of Huai'an, those provisions shall also apply.

articles.6:
The People's Government of Huai'an District shall organize and carry out protection work for the National Historic and Cultural City of Huai'an, take the lead in establishing a mechanism to advance that work, and perform the following duties:
(1) implement the conservation plan and prepare conservation plans for historic districts and historic areas;
(2) organize surveys of protected assets, prepare and publish the protection register, and keep it updated;
(3) prepare and implement annual projects and plans for protection, continuity, and use;
(4) publicize and implement laws and regulations on protection;
(5) conduct research, collect and organize information, provide education and training, and promote external exchanges concerning protection and use; and
(6) handle protection matters assigned by higher-level governments and relevant departments.
The People's Government of Huai'an District shall include protection funding in its fiscal budget, specify the protection responsibilities of its departments and of township people's governments and subdistrict offices, and carry out comprehensive administrative law enforcement in accordance with the law.

articles.12:
The following activities are prohibited within a protected area of the National Historic and Cultural City of Huai'an:
(1) damaging the traditional layout or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens and green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in the conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or similar facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, corrosive, or other hazardous substances;
(4) carving or defacing historic buildings, damaging their exterior, or altering it without authorization; or
(5) other activities prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.13:
Construction within the historic urban area shall comply with the conservation plan for the National Historic and Cultural City of Huai'an and the following requirements:
(1) protect the natural environment of the ‘Four Lakes and One Shoal’—Xiaohu, Shaohu, Yuehu, and Hehu Lakes and Taohuayin—and the ‘Three Linked Cities’ layout of the Old City, Liancheng, and the New City, as well as the ancient city's central axis and street and lane pattern; ensure compatibility with traditional spatial form and architectural character;
(2) encourage the use of traditional building techniques and craftsmanship to protect and repair traditional residences, seeking to repair them in their original style and preserve their existing character;
(3) separate stormwater and sewage, and connect sewage pipes to the municipal sewage collection system as required. Where the spatial constraints of streets and lanes make separation impossible, combined stormwater and sewage shall be discharged into the municipal sewage collection system and not into waterways; and
(4) do not construct, reconstruct, or expand buildings or structures in violation of the plan.

articles.14:
Construction within a historic district shall comply with the construction controls in the conservation plan and the following requirements:
(1) within the core conservation area, new construction and expansion are prohibited except for necessary infrastructure and public-service facilities;
(2) demolition of a building, structure, or other facility other than a historic building within the core conservation area shall follow the approval procedures prescribed by law;
(3) effective protective measures shall be taken when constructing roads, underground works, or other municipal infrastructure, so as not to damage protected assets;
(4) outdoor advertisements, signs, awnings, covered walkways, sanitation facilities, and other external facilities, and exterior decoration of buildings and structures, shall be compatible with the district's historic character; and
(5) new or renovated urban utility lines shall be buried underground. Where conditions make this impossible, they shall be concealed or technically treated to meet conservation requirements.

articles.8:
Protected assets of the historic and cultural city of Chuzhou mainly include:
(1) the Ancient City of Chuzhou and the Ancient City of Fengyang;
(2) historic districts, including North Main Street, Jingang Lane, and Zunyang Street in Chuzhou and the former site of the British-American Tobacco Company at Mentai in Fengyang;
(3) historic villages and traditional villages, including Longgang and Chahe Villages in Tianchang, Xiaogang Village in Fengyang, Meiying Village in Mingguang, and Taipingji Village in Nanqiao;
(4) historic areas, including Yunji Street in Fengyang, the Old Street of Luqiao Town in Dingyuan, the Old Wuyi Street in Nanqiao, the ancient village of Shijiepai in Lai'an, and Yuanjiawan Old Street in Quanjiao;
(5) immovable cultural relics, historic buildings, and traditional-character buildings, including Qingliuguan, Baizi Longtan, the old Chuzhou Station from the late Qing period and its important associated remains, and Taiping Bridge over the Chi River;
(6) intangible cultural heritage, including Fengyang Flower Drums, Fengyang folk songs, Tianchang filial-piety culture, and Quanjiao's Zou Taiping festival;
(7) historic river and lake systems, including Chuzhou West Ravine, Shangshuiguang, Xiashuiguang, South Lake, and North Lake, and ancient and notable trees, including the thousand-year-old ginkgo at Baoshan in Lai'an; and
(8) other protected matters prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.12:
Protection of the Ancient City of Chuzhou shall comply with its conservation plan and provide integrated protection that preserves its traditional layout, historic character, and spatial scale. The following requirements shall also be observed:
(1) protect the landscape-view corridor between the ancient city and Langya Mountain; strictly control the intensity of urban construction and building heights; and carry on the historic landscape described as ‘mountains surrounding Chuzhou’;
(2) preserve the original fabric, spatial structure, and appearance of historic streets and lanes, and protect the traditional courtyard spaces of courtyard-style residences. Street and lane repairs shall preserve the traditional layout and historic character;
(3) regulate household number plates, street and lane signs, and signs presenting historical and cultural information. Shop signs, markers, and similar facilities shall meet applicable requirements and be compatible with the landscape and environment; and
(4) protect and restore historic river and lake systems and their landscape character.

articles.13:
The following activities are prohibited within a protected area of a historic and cultural city:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage the traditional layout or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens and green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in the conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or similar facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, corrosive, or other hazardous substances;
(4) carving or defacing historic buildings or installing outdoor advertisements that do not meet planning requirements; or
(5) other conduct prohibited by laws or regulations.

articles.18:
A system of designated conservation responsibility shall apply to historic and cultural cities. The responsible person shall be determined as follows:
(1) for a historic district, historic town or village, traditional village, or historic area, the people's government of the county, county-level city, or district where it is located;
(2) for a historic building, its owner; if the owner cannot be located or ownership is unclear, its user;
(3) where a protected asset has a separate protection and administration entity, that entity; and
(4) where a protected asset crosses administrative boundaries, the people's government at the next higher common level shall designate the responsible person.
Where no responsible person can be identified, the Municipal People's Government shall designate one.

articles.19:
The person responsible for a historic district, historic town or village, traditional village, or historic area shall perform the following conservation duties in accordance with applicable requirements:
(1) preserve the integrity of the traditional layout and historic character of buildings and structures within the protected area;
(2) conduct routine inspections and promptly stop harmful conduct when discovered;
(3) keep the protected area clean and orderly;
(4) ensure the proper operation of public facilities and equipment for fire safety and disaster prevention; and
(5) perform other duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.20:
The person responsible for a historic building shall perform the following conservation duties in accordance with the conservation plan:
(1) carry out routine maintenance and repair and preserve the building's original exterior form and character;
(2) ensure structural safety, assist the relevant departments in keeping public fire-safety and disaster-prevention facilities and equipment operational, promptly address hazards when discovered, and report them to the relevant competent department;
(3) notify a transferee, lessee, or user in writing of the conservation responsibilities when transferring, leasing, or lending the building; and
(4) perform other duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.23:
The people's governments of the Municipality and its counties, county-level cities, and districts may take the following measures to promote reasonable use of protected assets:
(1) encourage organizations, social organizations, and individuals to participate in reasonable use through policy guidance, financial support, and lawful fee reductions or exemptions;
(2) encourage the organization and exploration of historical and cultural resources and support distinctive commercial or public-interest activities using those resources, including cultural creativity, cultural experiences, leisure tourism, traditional handicrafts, and dissemination of intangible cultural heritage;
(3) organize academic research, practical innovation, cultural outreach, and educational activities related to protection of historic and cultural cities, and support schools in providing related practical education; and
(4) encourage enterprises and public institutions, social organizations, and individuals to open eligible historic buildings to the public as public places and introduce cultural and service functions such as memorial halls, exhibition halls, and museums in a manner suited to the buildings.

articles.24:
Where a state organ or its staff member commits any of the following violations of these Regulations, the people's government of the Municipality or the relevant county, county-level city, or district, or the relevant administrative department, shall order correction and impose administrative liability on the person in charge and other persons directly responsible in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to organize preparation or amendment of a conservation plan, or designation or adjustment of the protection register, under the prescribed procedure;
(2) failing to perform approval duties under the prescribed procedure or in accordance with the conservation plan;
(3) failing to supervise and inspect the conservation status of protected assets in accordance with the law, or inadequately supervising approved alterations or uses of protected assets;
(4) unlawfully failing to accept a report or complaint or investigate unlawful conduct;
(5) abusing official authority, neglecting duties, or engaging in malpractice for personal gain; or
(6) committing another violation prescribed by laws or regulations.

articles.3:
Protection of the historic and cultural city shall highlight its cultural and natural character as a famous city of Chu and Han, a revolutionary landmark, a center of Huxiang culture, and a city of mountains, water, and river islands. The principal protected matters are:
(1) historic-character conservation areas, historic districts, towns, and villages;
(2) cultural relics, historic buildings, important historic sites and remains, and their compatible surroundings;
(3) scenic areas, old wells and springs, and ancient and notable trees;
(4) intangible cultural heritage, including traditional arts and culture, traditional crafts, time-honored brands, century-old prestigious schools, and historic place names; and
(5) other protected matters prescribed by laws, regulations, or rules.

articles.31:
The Municipal People's Government shall install commemorative signs at the following important historic sites and remains and may, where a site has exceptionally significant historical value, create conditions for its restoration:
(1) sites of major historical events, former residences of notable persons, and other important places of activity;
(2) important sites of administrative, military, cultural, or educational institutions or other organizations that had a major historical influence;
(3) sites of important historical interactions between China and foreign countries and long-term premises of important foreign institutions;
(4) sites of buildings, bridges, and influential garden structures representing the highest standards of a particular historical period;
(5) sites of major archaeological excavations or discoveries; and
(6) former locations of famous streets that no longer exist.
No organization or individual may damage or occupy commemorative facilities marking important historic sites or remains.

articles.18:
The following conduct is prohibited within an area covered by a conservation plan for the historic and cultural city:
(1) demolition or construction in violation of the conservation plan;
(2) changing land-use functions designated in the conservation plan;
(3) exceeding control indicators for building height or floor-area ratio, or violating requirements concerning building scale or colors; or
(4) other conduct inconsistent with the conservation plan.

articles.22:
Construction in a historic district shall comply with the conservation plan and the following requirements:
(1) comply with requirements for the layout, pattern, and character of courtyards, streets, and lanes designated for protection in the conservation plan;
(2) plant greenery using scientifically sound methods suited to Shenyang's geography and climate and the requirements of its landscape;
(3) conduct no construction in non-construction areas other than landscaping, roads, and installation of municipal utility lines; and
(4) provide and improve municipal infrastructure in the historic district in accordance with relevant conservation requirements and technical standards.

articles.31:
Where a state organ or its staff member commits any of the following violations of these Regulations, the people's government of the Municipality or the relevant district, county, or county-level city, or the relevant department, shall order correction and impose an administrative sanction in accordance with the law. Where the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) adjusting a conservation plan without authorization;
(2) approving without authorization the demolition, reconstruction, expansion, or relocation of a historic building;
(3) approving a construction project without complying with the conservation plan; or
(4) committing another act in violation of laws, regulations, or these Regulations.

articles.11:
A building or structure that has not been designated as an immovable cultural relic may be identified as a historic building if it meets any of the following criteria:
(1) its architectural style, structure, materials, construction techniques, or engineering methods have architectural-artistic features or scientific research value;
(2) it reflects the history, culture, or folk traditions of Hengyang and has characteristics of a particular period or locality;
(3) it is representative or typical of Hengyang's economic and social development;
(4) it is associated with a major historical event or notable historical person; or
(5) it has other historical, scientific, artistic, or social value.

articles.20:
No organization or individual may carry out any of the following activities within the protected area of a historic urban area, historic district, historic area, historic building, historic town or village, or traditional village:
(1) quarrying, sand mining, mining, or other activities that damage the traditional layout or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens and green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in the conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or similar facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, corrosive, or other hazardous substances;
(4) constructing buildings or structures that damage the traditional layout or historic character;
(5) carving or defacing historic buildings;
(6) damaging, repairing, relocating, or demolishing historic buildings without authorization; or
(7) other conduct prohibited by laws or regulations.

articles.23:
The people's governments of the Municipality and its counties, county-level cities, and districts shall encourage and support organizations and individuals in using historical and cultural resources reasonably through:
(1) establishing museums, exhibition halls, memorial halls, art galleries, libraries, science-education bases, cultural-creation bases, and youth education and experience bases;
(2) researching outstanding local traditional culture and presenting traditional theatre, storytelling and musical performance, film and television productions, and folk-culture experiences and displays;
(3) making, selling, and promoting Hengyang's renowned and high-quality products, folk handicrafts, and tourist souvenirs;
(4) operating homestays and distinctive restaurants;
(5) establishing venues to promote, exhibit, and transmit intangible cultural heritage;
(6) developing health and wellness, cultural tourism, rural tourism, educational study tours, and other industries; or
(7) carrying out other reasonable-use activities.
Original residents are encouraged to continue living at their original sites in accordance with conservation plans, improve their living and working conditions, and engage in local distinctive industries and related business activities.

articles.13:
The persons responsible for protection of historic and cultural cities, towns, and villages; historic districts and areas; and traditional villages shall perform the following duties:
(1) preserve traditional layouts, spatial scale, street and lane patterns, and historic character, and protect the integrity of associated natural landscapes and environmental features;
(2) improve infrastructure, public service facilities, and the living environment;
(3) keep the environment within protected areas clean and orderly;
(4) research, interpret, and present historical, cultural, and landscape values;
(5) take effective measures for fire prevention, theft prevention, disaster prevention and mitigation, and other safety work;
(6) organize routine inspections, promptly stop conduct endangering historical and cultural heritage, and notify the relevant departments; and
(7) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.14:
The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties:
(1) routinely maintain the historic building and its protection sign and carry out repairs in accordance with the law;
(2) preserve the building's exterior appearance, features embodying its value, and other such elements;
(3) ensure structural safety and the proper functioning of fire-prevention, disaster-prevention, and other facilities and equipment; promptly address hazards when discovered; and report them to the township people's government, subdistrict office, or relevant administrative department where the building is located;
(4) when leasing or lending the historic building, set out the protection responsibilities in writing with the lessee or user; and
(5) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.
The housing and urban-rural development department of the people's government of the county, county-level city, or district shall notify the responsible person in writing of the protection responsibilities and provide information and technical guidance on maintenance, repairs, emergency response, and related matters.

articles.15:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of historic and cultural cities, towns, and villages; historic districts and areas; and traditional villages:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) carving or defacing historic buildings;
(5) removing or altering elements or distinctive components that embody the core value of a historic building;
(6) damaging the building's principal load-bearing structure or otherwise endangering its structural safety; or
(7) other activities prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.20:
The people's governments of the Municipality and its counties, county-level cities, and districts shall strengthen research and interpretation of all types of historical and cultural heritage, explore its historical stories, cultural value, and spiritual meaning, plan uses suited to local characteristics and differences, develop routes presenting history and culture as part of production and daily life, and promote cultural tourism and related cultural industries.
Organizations and individuals are encouraged and supported in adaptively reusing historic towns and villages, historic districts and areas, traditional villages, historic buildings, and other assets. Multifunctional and sustainable adaptive reuse is encouraged through:
(1) establishing public cultural venues such as museums, exhibition halls, art galleries, and libraries;
(2) researching outstanding local traditional culture; presenting traditional opera and folk arts; filming audiovisual works; and providing experiences and presentations of folk cultural activities;
(3) producing, selling, and promoting locally renowned products, folk handicrafts, and tourist souvenirs;
(4) operating homestays and distinctive restaurants;
(5) establishing presentation and transmission venues to publicize, present, and teach intangible cultural heritage;
(6) developing health and wellness, cultural tourism, rural tourism, educational study tours, and other industries; and
(7) carrying out other activities conducive to effective inheritance and adaptive reuse.

articles.21:
The competent department for a protected asset shall establish an archive for that asset, manage it digitally, and maintain and update it dynamically. The archive shall include:
(1) materials obtained through surveys and designation;
(2) information on the asset's cultural and artistic features, historical characteristics, historical development, historical events, notable persons, technical particulars, and related matters;
(3) its current use and changes in ownership;
(4) conservation plans;
(5) design and survey materials;
(6) written, graphic, photographic, and audiovisual materials produced during repair, maintenance, relocation, or demolition; and
(7) other materials that need to be preserved.

articles.9:
Protected assets of the City include:
(1) the Old County and Imperial City historic districts;
(2) the historic town of Hexi;
(3) traditional villages such as Dahui and Kedai;
(4) protected cultural relic sites, including the Xiushan ancient architectural complex, Tonghai Confucian Temple, and Jukui Pavilion, as well as immovable cultural relics not yet designated and published as protected cultural relic sites;
(5) historic buildings such as the Zhou Ancestral Hall and Zeng Family Compound;
(6) former residences of notable persons, including the former residences of Ma Kechang and Zheng Kaiwen;
(7) revolutionary sites, including martyrs' cemeteries and the tomb of martyr Zhang Dun;
(8) valuable historic sites and remains from different historical periods, including the Muchengshan and Xingyi sites;
(9) historic environmental features such as ancient and notable trees, old roads, bridges, and wells;
(10) intangible cultural heritage, including Dongjing music (the Miaoshan Women's Dongjing Music), raised-pavilion performances (Tonghai high platforms), stone lions of southern Yunnan, and the repair and construction techniques for traditional timber-frame buildings;
(11) the integrated mountain, city, and lake landscape and natural setting formed by Xiushan, the Ancient City of Tonghai, and Qilu Lake; and
(12) other protected assets prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.12:
Persons responsible for protection of historic districts, historic towns, and traditional villages shall perform the following duties:
(1) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, distinctive decoration, spatial scale, principal view corridors, and historic environmental features;
(2) ensure the safety of protected assets and the proper functioning of public disaster-prevention, fire-prevention, and other facilities and equipment;
(3) conduct routine inspections and promptly stop and handle conduct that causes harm;
(4) keep the environment within protected areas clean and attractive; and
(5) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.17:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of the City:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) constructing buildings, structures, or other facilities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(5) damaging the integrity of the original architectural style, landscape, view corridors, or environment;
(6) damaging, relocating, or demolishing a historic building without authorization;
(7) removing or transferring doors, windows, plaques, signs, archways, or other decorative components of a historic building;
(8) carving or defacing a historic building;
(9) damaging, misappropriating, demolishing without authorization, or disabling firefighting facilities or equipment; or
(10) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.18:
The following activities are prohibited within core protected areas:
(1) using colored steel sheets, asbestos-cement sheets, resin tiles, or reflective materials on roofs or exterior walls, or installing solar-energy equipment, water tanks, water towers, exhaust pipes, television towers, communications or power equipment, or other facilities that affect historic character;
(2) installing or using rolling shutters or other facilities that affect historic character;
(3) placing or installing signs or awnings that are incompatible with historic character;
(4) using loudspeakers or other methods that repeatedly or continuously produce high noise levels for advertising or other activities;
(5) engaging in street trading or setting up stalls without authorization, or parking motor vehicles or non-motor vehicles in a disorderly manner;
(6) installing, moving, altering, or damaging signs without authorization; or
(7) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.24:
Organizations and individuals are encouraged and supported in carrying out the following activities:
(1) exploring the history and culture of the "Land of Rites and Music," couplets, and other heritage, and conducting cultural creativity and cultural experience activities;
(2) carrying on traditional handicrafts, including silver ornament forging, repair and construction of traditional timber-frame buildings, woodcarving, and stone carving, and developing, making, presenting, and promoting traditional handicrafts and cultural and creative products;
(3) establishing folk-custom transmission venues and researching and presenting intangible cultural heritage such as Dongjing music (the Miaoshan Women's Dongjing Music) and raised-pavilion performances (Tonghai high platforms);
(4) opening traditional handicraft workshops and carrying on distinctive food culture represented by cooking-pot dishes, bean-powder candy, sweet white wine, and Tonghai soy sauce; and
(5) organizing academic research, practical innovation, cultural publicity, and education related to protection of the City; exploring and studying its culture; promoting cultural exchanges and technological innovation; and training professional personnel.

articles.10:
Protected assets of the historic and cultural city include:
(1) important physical spaces formed during the early and middle periods of the Qing dynasty's consolidation and development of a unified, multi-ethnic State in China;
(2) the Chengde section of the Great Wall World Cultural Heritage, the Mountain Resort and its Outlying Temples World Cultural Heritage, the cultural corridor of the Luan River water-transport route, and sections of the imperial route taken by Qing emperors on their northern tours;
(3) the mountains and waters, spatial layout, historic terrain, traditional streets and lanes, important landscape viewpoints and view corridors, and cultural landscapes of the historic urban area closely related to the value of the historic and cultural city;
(4) historic districts;
(5) immovable cultural relics and historic buildings;
(6) intangible cultural heritage, outstanding traditional culture, and related objects and places;
(7) revolutionary historical sites, revolutionary traditions, and red resources embodying scenes and objects associated with the Saihanba spirit; and
(8) other assets that shall be protected in accordance with the law.

articles.11:
Key protected assets of the historic and cultural city include:
(1) World Cultural Heritage represented by the Mountain Resort of Chengde and its Outlying Temples;
(2) historic towns, historic villages, and traditional villages in Chengde, including Fengshan Town;
(3) Chengde's Erdaopailou, Luanhe Old Street, and Shouwangfen historic districts, and published historic areas;
(4) cultural relic protection units at all levels and immovable cultural relics not yet designated and published as such;
(5) the northern China's Danxia landform heritage represented by Qichui Peak and Shuangta Mountain;
(6) the cultural route of the Qing emperors' northern tours and remains of city sites; and
(7) other assets that shall be protected in accordance with the law.
Chapter III — Protection Measures

articles.17:
The person responsible for protection of the historic and cultural city shall perform the following duties in accordance with the law:
(1) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, distinctive decoration, spatial scale, and historic environmental features of buildings and structures within protected areas;
(2) ensure structural safety, promptly address hazards when discovered, and report them to the relevant departments;
(3) assist the relevant departments in ensuring the proper functioning of public fire-prevention, disaster-prevention, and other facilities and equipment;
(4) keep protected areas clean and attractive;
(5) carry out repairs in accordance with relevant provisions;
(6) conduct routine inspections, promptly stop conduct endangering protected assets, and notify the relevant departments of such conduct; and
(7) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.22:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of the historic and cultural city:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying roads, river and lake systems, gardens, green spaces, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) damaging, relocating, or demolishing a historic building without authorization;
(5) carrying out exterior repairs or decoration on a historic building, adding facilities, or changing its structure or use without approval; or
(6) damaging objects or places forming part of intangible cultural heritage.
Chapter IV — Rational Use

articles.26:
Organizations and individuals are encouraged and supported in making use of historical and cultural resources within historic urban areas, historic districts, historic towns and villages, traditional villages of China, and historic-building protected areas through the following means:
(1) establishing museums, exhibition halls, memorial halls, and similar venues;
(2) presenting folk arts and producing audiovisual works;
(3) establishing studios and transmission venues for representative inheritors of intangible cultural heritage to research and transmit such heritage;
(4) opening eligible historic buildings for public use;
(5) producing, presenting, and promoting traditional handicrafts;
(6) establishing educational and experiential bases for students; and
(7) conducting other public-interest activities for protection and use.
Chapter V — Legal Liability

articles.28:
Where a people's government of a county, county-level city, or district commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations, the Municipal People's Government shall order correction, and the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to organize preparation of a special conservation plan;
(2) failing to organize preparation of a special conservation plan in accordance with prescribed procedures;
(3) amending a special conservation plan without authorization; or
(4) failing to publish an approved special conservation plan.

articles.8:
Protected assets of the historic and cultural city of Xingtai include:
(1) the traditional layout and historic character of the historic urban area represented by the "one road, two cities, six wards" of the ancient city of Xingzhou;
(2) historic districts represented by North Street and Yangshidao; historic towns represented by Huangsi and Shahecheng; historic villages represented by Yingtan and Wangnao; traditional villages represented by Shentou and Fanxiacao; and historic-character areas represented by Mashijie;
(3) protected cultural relic sites represented by the Xing Kiln site, Puli Temple Pagoda, the Xingtai section of the Grand Canal, and the Dacheng Hall of Pingxiang Confucian Temple, and registered immovable cultural relics represented by the Gulu East site and stone statues in Nanhuangni Village;
(4) historic buildings represented by the residence at No. 19 Yangjiaxiang and the residence at No. 102 Dongdajie, and buildings with traditional character represented by Budai Courtyard and its architectural complex;
(5) modern and contemporary revolutionary historical sites represented by the former headquarters of the Eastern Advance Column of the 129th Division in Nangong and the site complex of the Anti-Japanese Military and Political University;
(6) ancient and notable trees represented by nationally designated first-grade ancient trees;
(7) intangible cultural heritage represented by Wang Qihe Taijiquan, Xingtai Plum Blossom Boxing, and Guangzong Daoist music;
(8) place-name cultural heritage represented by Xingxiang and Nanguan; and
(9) scenic areas, industrial heritage, agricultural cultural heritage, irrigation-engineering heritage, and other assets prescribed by laws and regulations.
Where the foregoing protected assets concern cultural relics, scenic areas, traditional villages, industrial heritage, ancient and notable trees, traditional place names, intangible cultural heritage, or other matters for which laws or regulations have special provisions, those provisions shall apply.

articles.17:
A conservation plan for a historic and cultural city, town, village, or district approved in accordance with the law shall not be amended without authorization. Where amendment is necessary due to any of the following circumstances, the authority that prepared the conservation plan shall submit a special report to the original approval authority and may prepare an amendment only after consent is obtained:
(1) an amendment or repeal of a law or regulation on which the plan is based affects its implementation;
(2) an important historical and cultural remain, such as an underground site, is newly discovered and amendment is necessary;
(3) a natural disaster, major accident, or other event causes a significant change to historical and cultural remains or their setting within the original protected area;
(4) an administrative-division adjustment makes amendment necessary;
(5) a major State construction project makes amendment necessary; or
(6) another circumstance for which the conservation plan shall be amended in accordance with the law.
An amended conservation plan shall be resubmitted for approval and publication in accordance with statutory procedures.

articles.20:
Persons responsible for protection of historic towns, villages, and districts shall perform the following duties in accordance with the law:
(1) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, street and lane patterns, historic character, spatial scale, and historic environmental features within protected areas;
(2) conduct routine inspections and promptly address conduct endangering protected assets;
(3) improve supporting services and facilities for the living environment and enhance the quality of historic spaces;
(4) carry out fire-safety, flood prevention and drainage, corrosion prevention, pest prevention, and related work; and
(5) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.21:
The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties in accordance with the law:
(1) routinely maintain and repair the historic building and preserve its original features, including height, bulk, internal structure, exterior appearance, and colors;
(2) ensure structural safety and the proper functioning of fire-prevention and disaster-prevention facilities, equipment, and access routes; promptly address hazards when discovered; and report them to the relevant departments;
(3) when transferring, leasing, or lending the historic building, clearly inform the transferee, lessee, or user of the building's protection and use requirements and legal liability; and
(4) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.32:
Where a relevant department of the Municipal People's Government or a people's government at the county level, or a relevant department thereof, commits any of the following acts, the people's government or relevant administrative department at the municipal or county level shall order correction; the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law; and if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to establish a protection list or propose an adjustment in accordance with statutory procedures or designation standards;
(2) failing to organize, or to organize in accordance with statutory procedures, the preparation, amendment, or publication of a conservation plan;
(3) failing to formulate a safeguard plan for roads, water supply and drainage, power, sanitation, fire safety, or other facilities as required for protection;
(4) failing to install protection signs as required;
(5) failing to perform supervision and administration duties or failing to investigate unlawful conduct when discovered; or
(6) other conduct violating laws or regulations concerning protection of the historic and cultural city.

articles.5:
A coordination mechanism for protection of the City shall be established and improved to coordinate the resolution of major protection issues. The County's Historic and Cultural City Protection and Administration Agency shall handle specific work and perform the following duties:
(1) lead preparation of special plans for protection and use of the City and study and formulate implementation plans;
(2) prepare plans for special protection funds together with relevant departments;
(3) organize routine protection and inspections and assist with law enforcement concerning protection of the City;
(4) organize development of the City's cultural brand, cultural exchanges, and research, and participate in tourism planning that uses the City's resources;
(5) guide designs for street and market landscapes and plans for construction projects concerning protection and adaptive reuse;
(6) collect and organize archives and materials concerning the City, establish an archival database, and administer it digitally; and
(7) perform other duties assigned by the County People's Government.
The agency shall establish an expert advisory mechanism to provide review, assessment, consultation, and other services on major protection matters.

articles.10:
The historic urban area is a key protected asset of the City. Protected features include:
(1) its historic site setting and natural character; its traditional layout of "a mountain commanding the city, oriented west to east, with mountains connected to water"; and its overall character reflecting multiple historical periods;
(2) historic streets and lanes, spatial scale, landscape setting, and historic character of the Shangshu Lane and Red Army Street historic districts;
(3) traditional character and local features of historic areas such as Qinglian Lane and Houfang Street; and
(4) other features requiring protection.

articles.17:
The following conduct is prohibited within the historic urban area:
(1) occupying or damaging ancient wells or the ancient city wall;
(2) occupying or damaging pavilions, covered walkways, archways, or their facilities;
(3) conducting business on roads without authorization;
(4) unlawfully discharging cooking fumes;
(5) keeping dogs in public places or common areas of buildings; or
(6) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.35:
Organizations and individuals are encouraged to make rational use of the City's resources, in accordance with the principle of necessity and proportionality and in compliance with the law, through projects and activities conducive to historical and cultural protection and inheritance, including:
(1) exploring, organizing, carrying on, and promoting traditional culture with local characteristics;
(2) publicizing, presenting, and teaching representative intangible cultural heritage, including Meilin Opera, Dayuan Nuo dance, and Shangqing ancient music, and presenting folk arts and cultural theme activities with local characteristics;
(3) establishing thematic museums, exhibition halls, memorial halls, art galleries, and research centers for notable historical figures and culture;
(4) researching, developing, and operating traditional food culture;
(5) developing, making, and selling art, souvenirs, and folk handicrafts with local characteristics and developing cultural tourism projects such as traditional workshops and educational study tours; and
(6) other projects and activities conducive to historical and cultural protection and inheritance.

articles.8:
[Protected assets] Protected assets of the historic and cultural city of this Municipality include:
(1) historic urban areas represented by the old Ming- and Qing-dynasty city area of Liaoyang and the former South Manchuria Railway affiliated area near the railway station, and the associated mountains and rivers;
(2) historic towns and historic villages;
(3) historic districts represented by the Baita and Tanghutun historic districts, and historic areas represented by Qingnian Street;
(4) protected cultural relic sites at all levels; immovable cultural relics registered but not yet designated as protected cultural relic sites; and underground archaeological deposit areas;
(5) historic buildings;
(6) industrial heritage;
(7) cultural routes and cultural heritage clusters;
(8) historic environmental features such as notable trees and ancient wells;
(9) intangible cultural heritage represented by traditional place names and Liaoning (Liaoyang) drum music; and
(10) other assets requiring protection.

articles.17:
[Duties of persons responsible for historic urban areas, historic towns and villages, historic districts, and historic areas] The persons responsible for protection of historic urban areas, historic towns and villages, historic districts, and historic areas shall perform the following duties:
(1) preserve the integrity of original traditional layouts, historic character, spatial scale, and associated natural landscapes and settings within protected areas;
(2) keep the overall environment within protected areas clean and attractive;
(3) conduct routine inspections, promptly stop conduct endangering historical and cultural heritage, and notify the relevant departments;
(4) take effective measures to improve fire prevention, disaster prevention, and related work; and
(5) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.18:
[Duties of persons responsible for historic buildings] The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties:
(1) routinely maintain and repair the building and preserve its original features, including height, bulk, internal structure, exterior appearance, and colors;
(2) ensure structural safety and the proper functioning of fire-prevention and disaster-prevention facilities, equipment, and access routes; promptly address hazards when discovered; and report them to the relevant departments;
(3) when transferring, leasing, or lending the building, the transferor, lessor, or lender shall clearly inform the transferee, lessee, or user of protection and use requirements and legal liability; and
(4) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.23:
[Historic-building archives] Within one year after historic buildings are published as designated, the housing and urban-rural development departments of the people's governments of the Municipality and its counties, county-level cities, and districts shall establish and administer archives for them and incorporate the archives into the information platform for protection of the historic and cultural city.
Historic-building archives shall include:
(1) the buildings' artistic and historical features, dates of construction, and rarity;
(2) relevant technical materials;
(3) current use and changes in ownership;
(4) written, graphic, photographic, and audiovisual materials produced during repair, decoration, and renovation;
(5) survey records and related materials; and
(6) other materials that need to be preserved.
Persons responsible for protection of historic buildings shall assist with archival surveys and building surveys.

articles.35:
[Legal liability of people's governments and departments] Where a people's government of a county-level city or district commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations, the Municipal People's Government shall order correction, and the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to organize preparation of a conservation plan;
(2) failing to organize preparation of a conservation plan in accordance with statutory procedures;
(3) amending a conservation plan without authorization; or
(4) failing to publish an approved conservation plan.

articles.11:
Key protected assets to be included in the conservation plan for the historic and cultural city include:
(1) traditional layouts and historic character of the historic urban area;
(2) historic districts such as Xinghua Prefecture, Luomutian, and Yuyading, and historic-character areas such as Dongyan Mountain, Guanghua Temple, and the ancient city of Puxi;
(3) historic towns and villages and traditional villages;
(4) historic buildings, buildings with traditional character, and immovable cultural relics such as Mulanpi, Xianyou Confucian Temple, and the site of the Southern Shaolin Temple;
(5) scenic areas and nature reserves such as Meizhou Island and Jiuli Lake;
(6) industrial heritage, agricultural cultural heritage, important revolutionary historical sites, and historical sites concerning Taiwan and overseas Chinese;
(7) historic environmental features such as ancient rivers and lakes and ancient lychee trees;
(8) intangible cultural heritage, Mazu culture, and other outstanding traditional Putian and Xianyou cultures; and
(9) other protected assets prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.19:
The person responsible for protection of a historic urban area or historic district shall be determined according to the jurisdiction of the people's government of the Municipality or the relevant county or district and shall perform the following duties:
(1) implement conservation-plan requirements;
(2) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, spatial scale, and historic environmental features within protected areas;
(3) improve public services, municipal transport, urban-safety facilities, and the living environment;
(4) research and present historical, cultural, and landscape values;
(5) conduct routine inspections and promptly address harmful conduct;
(6) carry out fire prevention, theft prevention, disaster prevention, and other safety work; and
(7) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.
Historic towns, villages, and traditional villages shall be protected under territorial administration, and protection duties shall be performed in accordance with relevant responsibilities.

articles.21:
The following conduct is prohibited within protected areas of historic and cultural cities, districts, towns, villages, and traditional villages:
(1) quarrying, sand mining, mining, clearing forests for cultivation, or other conduct that damages traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, ancient and notable trees, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, docks, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) constructing buildings, structures, or other facilities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(5) installing advertisements, signs, or posters that damage or affect the area's character;
(6) dumping garbage, discharging sewage, or otherwise polluting the environment;
(7) damaging objects or places forming part of intangible cultural heritage; or
(8) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.6:
Protection funding for the historic and cultural city may be used for:
(1) preparation and amendment of conservation plans for the historic and cultural city and plans for repair of related protected cultural relic sites and historic buildings;
(2) subsidies for repair of immovable cultural relics and historic buildings;
(3) subsidies for surveys, collection, protection, and transmission of intangible cultural heritage and outstanding traditional culture; and
(4) other expenditures for protection of the historic and cultural city.

articles.13:
The Municipal People's Government may designate a building or structure that has not been designated as an immovable cultural relic as a historic building if it meets any of the following conditions:
(1) it reflects Yueyang's history, culture, or folk traditions and has distinctive features of a particular period and region;
(2) its architectural style, structure, materials, or engineering techniques reflect regional culture or artistic features or have scientific research value;
(3) it is associated with an important historical event in politics, the economy, culture, or military affairs, or with a notable historical figure;
(4) it is a modern building or industrial heritage that bears witness to the development of this Municipality and has local characteristics; or
(5) it is another building or structure with historical or cultural significance.

articles.16:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of the historic and cultural city:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances; or
(4) carving or defacing a historic building or installing outdoor advertisements that do not meet planning requirements.

articles.33:
Where the people's governments of the Municipality or its districts or their staff members commit any of the following acts, the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law; if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to include protection funding in the fiscal budget at the same level as required by Article 5;
(2) failing to organize public education on protection of the historic and cultural city as required by Article 8;
(3) failing to supervise, inspect, and assess protection as required by Article 9;
(4) failing to organize preparation of conservation plans and repair plans for historic buildings as required by the first paragraph of Article 15;
(5) failing to arrange installation of protection signs as required by Article 29;
(6) failing to strengthen protection of ancient and notable trees within their jurisdictions as required by Article 32; or
(7) other dereliction of duty, abuse of authority, or malpractice for personal gain.

articles.34:
Where a relevant administrative department or its staff member commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations, the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law; if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) the housing and urban-rural development department fails to designate or adjust a historic-character protected area or historic building in accordance with the procedure in Article 14;
(2) the housing and urban-rural development department fails to submit for approval, in accordance with the procedure in the third paragraph of Article 28, a historic building that must be relocated or demolished;
(3) the competent cultural relics department fails to perform its duties to protect immovable cultural relics within protected areas as required by the second paragraph of Article 4 and Articles 22 and 23;
(4) the culture and tourism department fails to perform its duties to protect and transmit intangible cultural heritage and outstanding traditional culture within protected areas as required by the third paragraph of Article 4 and Articles 30 and 31;
(5) the housing and urban-rural development department fails to supervise and administer repair and safety of use of historic buildings within protected areas as required by the first paragraph of Article 4;
(6) relevant departments of development and reform, finance, natural resources and planning, urban management and comprehensive law enforcement, forestry, industry and information technology, water affairs, civil affairs, and other matters fail to cooperate in protection within their respective responsibilities as required by the fourth paragraph of Article 4; or
(7) other dereliction of duty, abuse of authority, or malpractice for personal gain.

articles.8:
Protected assets of the historic and cultural city include:
(1) historic urban areas;
(2) historic districts;
(3) immovable cultural relics;
(4) Great Wall cultural sites;
(5) ancient irrigation canals such as the Qin Canal, Han Canal, Hanyan Canal, and Tanglai Canal;
(6) historic buildings;
(7) traditional villages;
(8) ancient and notable trees;
(9) historic place names;
(10) intangible cultural heritage; and
(11) other protected assets prescribed by laws and regulations.
Where protection of the foregoing assets concerns cultural relics, the Great Wall, ancient irrigation canals, ancient and notable trees, historic place names, intangible cultural heritage, and related matters, the Law of the People's Republic of China on the Protection of Cultural Relics and its implementing regulations, the Law of the People's Republic of China on Intangible Cultural Heritage, the State Council's Regulations on the Protection of the Great Wall, the Regulations of the Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region on the Protection of the Yellow River Diversion Ancient Irrigation District World Heritage Irrigation Structures, the Regulations of the Ningxia Hui Autonomous Region on Place Names, the Regulations of Yinchuan Municipality on the Protection and Administration of Ancient and Notable Trees, and other applicable laws and regulations shall apply.

articles.12:
The Municipal People's Government shall organize preparation of a conservation plan for the historic and cultural city and submit it to the people's government of the autonomous region for approval, and file it with the housing and urban-rural development and cultural relics departments of the State Council.
A conservation plan for the historic and cultural city shall include:
(1) protection principles, protected assets, and protected areas;
(2) protection measures, development intensity, and construction controls;
(3) requirements for protection of traditional layouts and historic character;
(4) core protected areas and construction control areas for historic districts and historic buildings; and
(5) phased implementation plans for protection.

articles.20:
Persons responsible for protection of historic urban areas and historic districts shall, in accordance with conservation plans, perform the following duties:
(1) organize routine inspections, promptly stop conduct endangering protected assets of the historic and cultural city;
(2) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, distinctive decoration, spatial scale, and historic environmental features of buildings and structures within protected areas;
(3) improve infrastructure and public service facilities in historic urban areas and historic districts, optimize the living environment, carry out environmental improvements, and keep the areas clean and attractive;
(4) formulate measures to improve disaster prevention and mitigation in historic urban areas and historic districts and assist the relevant departments in ensuring the proper functioning of public fire-prevention, disaster-prevention, and other safety facilities and equipment; and
(5) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws, regulations, or the Municipal People's Government.

articles.21:
The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties:
(1) preserve traditional layout and historic character, including the building's original height, bulk, exterior appearance, distinctive decoration, and colors;
(2) ensure building safety and the proper functioning of fire-prevention and disaster-prevention facilities and equipment; promptly address safety hazards or emergencies when discovered; and report them to the housing and urban-rural development, fire and rescue, emergency management, and other competent departments where the building is located;
(3) keep the building clean and attractive;
(4) use, make use of, maintain, and repair the building in accordance with the plan for its protection and use and these Regulations; and
(5) comply with other requirements prescribed by laws and regulations.
Before transferring or leasing a historic building, the transferor or lessor shall inform the transferee or lessee of protection and repair requirements, specify the protection and repair obligations in the contract, and file the contract with the natural resources department of the county, county-level city, or district where the building is located. That department shall promptly transmit the filing information to the housing and urban-rural development and cultural relics departments at the same level.

articles.24:
The following activities are prohibited within a core protected area of a historic building:
(1) carving or defacing a building or structure;
(2) storing explosive or corrosive substances inside a building or structure;
(3) carrying out exterior repairs or decoration, adding facilities, or changing the structure or use of a historic building without authorization;
(4) damaging a building's principal load-bearing structure or otherwise endangering its safety; or
(5) other conduct affecting protection of the historic building.
If an organization or individual genuinely needs to carry out exterior repairs or decoration, add facilities, or change the structure or use of a historic building within its core protected area, it shall prepare an implementation plan in accordance with the plan for protection and use of the historic building. After review and consent by the natural resources, housing and urban-rural development, and cultural relics departments of the Municipality, the plan shall be submitted to the Municipal People's Government for approval, and related procedures shall be completed in accordance with laws and regulations.

articles.35:
Where a people's government of a county, county-level city, or district; a relevant department of a people's government of the Municipality or a county, county-level city, or district; or a township people's government or subdistrict office commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations, the people's governments of the Municipality and the relevant county, county-level city, or district shall, within their respective administrative authority, order correction and issue a public criticism. The persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to organize preparation, amendment, or publication of a conservation plan for a historic district or a plan for protection and use of historic buildings as required;
(2) failing to perform protection duties under Article 20;
(3) failing to perform duties under Articles 23, 24, or 26 in accordance with conservation plans or statutory procedures; or
(4) failing to install signs for historic districts or protection signs for historic buildings as required.

articles.11:
The Municipal People's Government shall establish a protection list for the historic and cultural city.
The list shall include:
(1) the Beiguan Historic District, historic urban area, and historic-character areas;
(2) historic buildings;
(3) underground archaeological deposit areas, ancient sites, guild halls, money shops, old residences, time-honored brands, ancient and notable trees, ancient wells, and ancient wine fermentation pits;
(4) traditional place names;
(5) intangible cultural heritage;
(6) protected cultural relic sites approved and published by the people's governments of the State, province, or Municipality, which shall be included directly in the list; and
(7) other protected assets designated by the Municipal People's Government for inclusion in the list.

articles.12:
The Municipal People's Government may designate a building or structure more than 50 years old as a historic building if it has not been published as a protected cultural relic site or registered as an immovable cultural relic and meets any of the following conditions:
(1) it has historical or cultural value;
(2) it has architectural or artistic value;
(3) it embodies scientific or technological value; or
(4) it has other distinctive value.
A building or structure more than 30 years old may be designated as a historic building if it has special historical, scientific, or artistic value or commemorative significance.

articles.31:
The people's governments of the Municipality and its districts may take the following measures to promote rational use of protected assets:
(1) support rational use through financial assistance, lawful fee reductions or exemptions, rewards for development rights, and other measures;
(2) establish management and operating enterprises or organizations in accordance with the law to integrate, explore, and publicize historical and cultural resources;
(3) acquire ownership or use rights in non-state-owned protected cultural relic sites or historic buildings by acquisition, exchange of property rights, lease, trusteeship, or other means; enterprises, social organizations, and individuals are encouraged to make rational use of such assets by lawful purchase or, without changing ownership, by acquisition, exchange, lease, trusteeship, or other means;
(4) encourage state organs, enterprises, public institutions, grassroots self-governing organizations, and social organizations to open eligible historic buildings to the public; and
(5) support representative inheritors of representative intangible cultural heritage items in conducting transmission and presentation activities within historic districts, immovable cultural relics, and historic buildings.

articles.34:
Where a state organ or its staff member commits any of the following acts, the people's government at the next higher level shall order correction; the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law; and if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to establish a protection list for the historic and cultural city as required;
(2) failing to organize preparation of a conservation plan;
(3) failing to organize preparation of a conservation plan in accordance with statutory procedures;
(4) amending a conservation plan without authorization;
(5) failing to publish an approved conservation plan;
(6) failing to install protection signs as required;
(7) failing to formulate special measures for roads, water supply, drainage, power, sanitation, fire safety, and other matters as required;
(8) failing to perform supervision and administration duties or failing to investigate unlawful conduct when discovered; or
(9) other unlawful circumstances prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.5:
The Historic and Cultural City Protection Committee shall perform the following duties:
(1) publicize and implement relevant laws and regulations;
(2) supervise specific implementation of the conservation plan and detailed control plans for the City;
(3) study and formulate measures and implementation plans for protection of the old city and participate in review of major projects;
(4) organize preparation and amendment of guidelines for building repair, repair plans, guidelines for advertisements, plans for development of business uses, and the protection list for the City;
(5) guide, coordinate, and urge relevant entities to protect, administer, and make rational use of the City;
(6) assist relevant departments with protection, transmission, and presentation of intangible cultural heritage of the City;
(7) organize research into traditional culture of the City and conduct academic research, external publicity, and exchanges; and
(8) perform other duties assigned by the County People's Government.

articles.14:
Protected assets of the City include:
(1) the overall layout and character of the historic urban area formed by the Yitong River, Jinzhong Mountain, the Confucian Temple area, the Great Buddha Temple area, historic streets and lanes inside and outside the old city, contiguous traditional residential courtyards, guild halls, temples, the mint, and other features;
(2) the overall layout and character of Baiwu Village formed by its old and notable residences, guild halls, ancestral halls, temples, and other assets;
(3) protected cultural relic sites; immovable cultural relics not yet designated and published as protected cultural relic sites; historic buildings; and the mountains, waters, forests, fields, and lakes closely related to the City's layout;
(4) ancient sites, wells, steles, lanes, bridges, doors and windows, plaques, shop platforms, screen walls, fences, archways, former residences of notable persons, ancient and notable trees, historic place names, names of historic buildings, time-honored commercial brands, and other assets;
(5) intangible cultural heritage such as mottled-copper craft, earthenware craft, Dongjing music, and lantern songs;
(6) copper-trading culture; and
(7) other cultural heritage with protection value.

articles.17:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of the City:
(1) constructing buildings, structures, or other facilities incompatible with the overall character or construction style, or damaging the integrity of original architectural styles, landscapes, view corridors, or the environment;
(2) damaging or demolishing without authorization a historic building designated for protection;
(3) removing ancient doors, windows, plaques, screen walls, fences, shop platforms, archways, or other decorative components from historic buildings or structures without authorization;
(4) occupying or damaging gardens, green spaces, ancient and notable trees, ancient wells, steles, lanes, bridges, water areas, or roads;
(5) carving or defacing historic buildings or structures, or moving or removing protection signs;
(6) occupying or damaging historic remains or sites;
(7) carrying out exterior repairs or decoration on historic buildings, traditional residences, shops, and similar structures, or changing courtyard layouts or structures, without authorization;
(8) damaging, moving, or removing public facilities without authorization;
(9) constructing modern buildings, structures, or other facilities over rivers;
(10) occupying, filling, covering, blocking, or cutting off rivers or ponds, or changing their natural banks, slopes, width, depth, course, or other features;
(11) excavating, widening, straightening, or otherwise altering streets and roads, or blasting, removing soil, dredging sand, quarrying, or excavating underground space without authorization;
(12) constructing facilities that endanger the safety of the City or pollute the environment;
(13) releasing Kongming lanterns; or
(14) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.18:
The following activities are prohibited within a core protected area:
(1) using reflective materials on roofs or exterior walls, or installing solar panels, water tanks, water towers, chimneys, television towers, or other facilities that affect the character of the City without authorization;
(2) placing or installing signs or awnings incompatible with the character of the City;
(3) installing or using rolling shutters or other facilities that affect the overall character;
(4) setting off fireworks and firecrackers;
(5) drilling wells to extract water; or
(6) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.28:
Citizens, legal persons, and other organizations are encouraged and supported in carrying out the following activities:
(1) researching and transmitting copper-trading culture and intangible cultural heritage, producing audiovisual works, developing cultural and creative industries, and establishing cultural research, training, and education bases;
(2) organizing research, creation, and presentation of literature, art, martial arts, and folk culture with local characteristics;
(3) producing and selling distinctive tourist souvenirs and traditional specialty products;
(4) developing, collecting, presenting, and trading folk handicrafts;
(5) establishing museums on the history, culture, and folk customs of Huize;
(6) providing technical support, policy publicity, volunteer interpretation, and other services for protection of the City; and
(7) other activities conducive to protection of the City and inheritance and dissemination of history and culture.

articles.31:
Where a staff member of a state organ or related entity of the Qujing Municipal People's Government or the People's Government of Huize County commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations, correction shall be ordered and the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible shall be disciplined in accordance with the law; if the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) adjusting, changing, or refusing to implement an approved conservation plan or detailed plan without authorization;
(2) failing to perform protection, supervision, or administration duties;
(3) failing to investigate conduct damaging protection of the City in accordance with the law; or
(4) other dereliction of duty, abuse of authority, or malpractice for personal gain.

articles.12:
The conservation plan for Xi'an, a historic and cultural city, shall emphasize protection of the intact layout of the Ming city and presentation of the grand scale of the Tang city, and strengthen protection and administration of major Zhou, Qin, Han, and Tang sites. It shall also meet the following requirements:
(1) coordinate with plans for national economic and social development, the overall urban plan, and the overall land-use plan;
(2) emphasize protection of the old city's historic character, urban layout, and spatial environment and reflect its distinctive character;
(3) strictly control building height, bulk, color, and style in accordance with protection requirements; and
(4) meet the needs of urban residents for a modern living and working environment.

articles.8:
Protected areas of Xiamen, a historic and cultural city, cover the entire administrative area of this Municipality. Protected assets include:
(1) World Cultural Heritage;
(2) historic urban areas; historic districts, towns, and villages; traditional villages; and historic areas;
(3) immovable cultural relics, historic buildings, and buildings with traditional character;
(4) industrial heritage, agricultural cultural heritage, and irrigation-engineering heritage;
(5) intangible cultural heritage and place-name cultural heritage; and
(6) other protected assets approved and published by the State, province, or Municipality.

articles.9:
Xiamen, a historic and cultural city, shall be protected as an organic whole. Its traditional layout and historic character shall be preserved and carried forward, and the authenticity and integrity of its historical and cultural heritage maintained. Priority shall be given to protecting:
(1) the overall bay-city layout of "mountains, sea, islands, and city";
(2) the organic whole of Gulangyu's historic built environment, formed by core cultural heritage features, cultural relics, buildings, ancient and notable trees, and island landscapes, together with the diverse cultural traditions extending through it;
(3) the overall silver-ingot-shaped layout of the ancient city of Tong'an, embraced by the two streams, and the spatial layout of the modern historic urban area formed along both shores of the Lujiang River and facing the sea;
(4) the spatial scale, historic street and lane patterns, and landscape character of historic districts and historic areas, and the Minnan traditional courtyard houses of traditional villages;
(5) historic architectural character represented by Minnan red-brick buildings, buildings in Xiamen's decorative style, Jiageng buildings, and arcaded buildings;
(6) intangible cultural heritage reflecting the history and culture of Xiamen, including Minnan traditional culture and arts, folk customs, and folk techniques; and
(7) other key protected assets designated by the people's governments of the Municipality and its districts.

articles.20:
The people's government of each district shall designate a person responsible for protection of a historic district, town, or village; traditional village; or historic area.
The responsible person shall perform the following duties:
(1) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, distinctive decoration, spatial scale, and historic environmental features of buildings and structures within protected areas;
(2) conduct routine inspections, promptly stop conduct endangering protected assets, handle it in accordance with the law, or notify the relevant administrative department;
(3) work with relevant departments to ensure the proper functioning of public fire-prevention, disaster-prevention, and other facilities and equipment and carry out fire-safety, flood prevention and drainage, geological disaster prevention, and related work; and
(4) meet other requirements established by the people's governments of the Municipality and its districts.

articles.18:
The protected water area of the Tuojiang River comprises the river section from Changtangang to Guanzhuang and the moat.
The waters of the Tuojiang River and scenery along both banks shall be strictly protected. View corridors along the river shall be preserved, and the water shall be kept clean and sanitary.
The following activities are prohibited within the protected water area of the Tuojiang River:
(1) engaging in industries that pollute the water;
(2) conducting business on the water without authorization;
(3) discharging wastewater into the river or dumping solid waste;
(4) poisoning, blasting, or electrocuting fish;
(5) establishing pollution-causing projects; or
(6) other activities that pollute the water or damage aquatic ecosystems.

articles.32:
Where any of the following circumstances occurs within protected areas of Fenghuang, a historic and cultural city, the protection and administration agency shall stop the conduct. If the circumstances are serious, the relevant departments shall revoke the business qualification in accordance with the law:
(1) conducting business outside the designated location;
(2) soliciting, pressuring, or cheating tourists through any means;
(3) conducting business on roads or as a mobile vendor;
(4) engaging in improper pricing; or
(5) other conduct violating laws, regulations, or these Regulations.

articles.17:
Persons responsible for protection of historic urban areas and historic districts shall perform the following duties:
(1) implement conservation-plan requirements;
(2) preserve the integrity of traditional layouts, historic character, spatial scale, and historic environmental features within protected areas;
(3) improve public services, municipal transport, urban-safety facilities, and the living environment;
(4) research and present historical, cultural, and landscape values;
(5) conduct routine inspections and promptly address harmful conduct; and
(6) carry out fire prevention, theft prevention, disaster prevention, and other safety work.

articles.18:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of historic urban areas and historic districts:
(1) quarrying, sand mining, mining, clearing forests for cultivation, or other conduct that damages traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, ancient and notable trees, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, docks, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) constructing buildings, structures, or other facilities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(5) installing advertisements, signs, or posters that damage or affect the area's character;
(6) dumping garbage, discharging wastewater, or otherwise polluting the environment;
(7) damaging objects or places forming part of intangible cultural heritage; or
(8) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.23:
The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties:
(1) routinely maintain and repair it and preserve its original traditional layout and historic character;
(2) ensure structural safety and the proper functioning of fire-prevention, disaster-prevention, and other facilities and equipment; promptly address hazards when discovered; and report them to the people's government where the building is located, the housing and urban-rural development or emergency management department, or a fire and rescue agency;
(3) when transferring, leasing, or lending the building, enter into a written agreement with the transferee, lessee, or user specifying each party's protection responsibilities; and
(4) perform other protection duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.40:
Where a person commits any of the following acts in violation of Article 18, the competent departments for urban management, culture and tourism, ecology and environment, and other matters shall, within their respective authority, order the person to stop the unlawful conduct and, within a specified period, restore the original condition or take other remedial measures. If the conduct causes serious consequences, the organization shall also be fined not less than RMB 50,000 but not more than RMB 200,000, and the individual shall also be fined not less than RMB 10,000 but not more than RMB 50,000. Any unlawful proceeds shall be confiscated. Any loss caused shall be compensated for in accordance with the law. If the conduct constitutes a crime, criminal liability shall be pursued in accordance with the law:
(1) constructing buildings, structures, or other facilities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) installing advertisements, signs, or posters that damage or affect the area's character;
(3) dumping garbage, discharging wastewater, or otherwise polluting the environment; or
(4) damaging objects or places forming part of intangible cultural heritage.

articles.3:
Protected areas of the City mainly include:
(1) the Ancient City of Dali;
(2) Xizhou Ancient Town and Longwei Pass Historic District;
(3) Shuanglang Historic Town and Zhoucheng Historic Village;
(4) protected areas and construction control areas for municipal- and higher-level protected cultural relic sites, such as the Three Pagodas of Chongsheng Temple, the Taihe City site (including the Nanzhao Stele of Dehua), and the Stele Commemorating Kublai Khan's Pacification of Yunnan, and for historic buildings; and
(5) the areas of Cangshan Mountain and Erhai Lake Scenic Area within the administrative area of Dali City (the "City Municipality").

articles.7:
The Ancient City of Dali Protection and Administration Agency of the People's Government of the City Municipality shall specifically protect the City within the four boundaries of the remains of the walls of the Ancient City of Dali and perform the following duties:
(1) publicize and implement relevant laws and regulations and these Regulations;
(2) organize implementation of plans and measures for protection of the ancient city;
(3) administer landscaping, sanitation, and the appearance of the ancient city;
(4) construct and maintain public infrastructure in the ancient city;
(5) provide review opinions on construction projects in the ancient city;
(6) supervise and inspect fire-safety work and implement fire-safety responsibilities;
(7) exercise certain administrative penalty powers in a relatively centralized manner within the approved scope; and
(8) perform other work related to protection of the ancient city.

articles.14:
Protected assets of the City include:
(1) valuable historic remains from all historical periods dating from the Neolithic Age onward;
(2) historic districts, towns, and villages and traditional villages;
(3) cultural relics;
(4) historic buildings and former residences of notable persons;
(5) revolutionary memorial sites;
(6) historic streets and lanes and traditional place names;
(7) ancient roads, bridges, wells, and ancient and notable trees;
(8) intangible cultural heritage;
(9) the natural landscape and setting of Cangshan Mountain and Erhai Lake Scenic Area; and
(10) other protected assets prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.30:
The following activities are prohibited within protected areas of the City:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) damaging, relocating, or demolishing a historic building without authorization;
(5) dismantling or transferring components of a historic building;
(6) carving or defacing a historic building;
(7) installing, moving, altering, or damaging protection signs without authorization;
(8) dumping or discharging wastewater into public areas or water bodies, or discarding garbage, feces, animal carcasses, or other waste;
(9) damaging streetlights, publicity boards, or other municipal facilities, damaging green spaces, or cutting down street trees; or
(10) other conduct prohibited by laws and regulations.

articles.11:
Protection objectives for the historic urban area include:
(1) preserving its authentic and intact historic-site setting and associated mountains and rivers;
(2) preserving its historic character, spatial scale, and traditional layout of "nine mountains embracing one city, with one river dividing the prefecture and garrison cities";
(3) preserving and carrying forward the original road-network layout and protecting the original spatial scale and street-facing character of traditional streets and lanes;
(4) strengthening construction controls along the Wuyang River historic landscape corridor and protecting the river itself, revetments, wharves, bridges, and waterfront buildings by feature;
(5) reasonably controlling population in the historic urban area, improving residents' quality of life, and maintaining vitality;
(6) optimizing land uses and improving public services and municipal infrastructure;
(7) developing green transport centered on public transit and supported by slow, pedestrian, and distinctive transport;
(8) improving integrated flood control and drainage, fire-safety, earthquake-resistance, and other disaster-prevention systems in accordance with applicable technical standards and specifications;
(9) strengthening protection of green spaces and landscape and improving vegetation; and
(10) protecting the ancient walls of the prefecture and garrison cities and carrying out routine maintenance and improvements to surrounding areas.

articles.15:
The following activities are prohibited within the historic urban area:
(1) quarrying, mining, or other activities that damage traditional layouts or historic character;
(2) occupying gardens, green spaces, river and lake systems, roads, or other areas designated for retention in a conservation plan;
(3) constructing factories, warehouses, or other facilities for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances;
(4) constructing overhead utility lines, elevated roads, interchanges, or freight hubs; or
(5) other activities affecting or damaging the City's traditional layout and historic character.

articles.17:
In addition to the prohibitions in Articles 15 and 16, the following activities are prohibited within a historic district:
(1) new construction or extension in a core protected area, except for necessary infrastructure and public service facilities;
(2) large-scale commercial development;
(3) keeping chickens, dogs, or other livestock in public places; or
(4) conducting business on roads in a manner that adversely affects the district environment.

articles.26:
The person responsible for protection of a historic building shall perform the following duties:
(1) use the building rationally in accordance with its protection requirements and function;
(2) preserve its original height, bulk, exterior appearance, and colors;
(3) preserve the integrity of its traditional layout, historic character, distinctive decoration, and historic environmental features;
(4) strengthen safety management, provide facilities and equipment for fire prevention, theft prevention, and protection from natural damage in accordance with relevant State provisions, and ensure the building's safety;
(5) strengthen maintenance and repair, promptly eliminate risks when the building is at risk of damage, and report them to the relevant competent departments; and
(6) perform other duties prescribed by laws and regulations.

articles.28:
The following conduct damaging a historic building is prohibited:
(1) excavating, damaging, occupying, relocating, or demolishing it without authorization;
(2) producing, storing, operating, or using explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances in it;
(3) carving or defacing it;
(4) installing outdoor advertisements on it without authorization;
(5) installing, moving, altering, or damaging its protection sign without authorization; or
(6) other conduct damaging its traditional character or structural safety.
Chapter IV — Ecological and Environmental Protection

articles.35:
The following activities are prohibited within the environmental coordination area:
(1) quarrying, mining, soil removal, kiln construction, or tomb construction;
(2) occupying or damaging gardens, green spaces, or river systems;
(3) establishing a garbage dump or spoil-disposal site in violation of relevant requirements;
(4) constructing projects that pollute the environment or damage ecosystems;
(5) constructing factories or warehouses for producing or storing explosive, flammable, radioactive, toxic, or corrosive substances; or
(6) other activities affecting or damaging the ecological environment.

articles.41:
The following lawful activities within the City are guided, encouraged, and supported:
(1) establishing bases for audiovisual production, literary and artistic creation, and transmission of folk arts and developing distinctive cultural and creative industries;
(2) holding traditional cultural festivals and events, including dragon-boat races, the Zhenyuan Lantern Festival, Zhenyuan Miao drum-step dance, the Zhenyuan Baojing "Third Day of the Third Month" festival, and the Zhenyuan Tujia "Eighth Day of the Eighth Month" festival;
(3) opening museums, exhibition halls, memorial halls, and venues for trading or presenting artworks and folk collections;
(4) operating distinctive inns, homestays, and shops;
(5) developing and operating distinctive Zhenyuan snacks and traditional restaurants;
(6) carrying on and presenting traditional techniques such as Baojing Dong embroidery and Zhenyuan woodcarving and producing and selling folk crafts, handicrafts, tourist souvenirs, and related products;
(7) exploring and carrying on intangible cultural heritage such as historical legends, cultural stories, and traditional customs of the City; and
(8) other activities that use the City's resources to carry on outstanding traditional culture.
Chapter VI — Legal Liability

articles.42:
Where a state organ or its staff member commits any of the following acts in violation of these Regulations and the conduct does not constitute a crime, the people's government of the Autonomous Prefecture or Zhenyuan County or a relevant competent department shall order correction and discipline the persons directly in charge and other persons directly responsible in accordance with the law:
(1) failing to organize, or to organize in accordance with statutory procedures, the preparation, submission for approval, amendment, or publication of a conservation plan;
(2) failing to perform approval duties in accordance with statutory procedures;
(3) failing to conduct routine inspections and protection of historic districts, historic buildings, or buildings with traditional character as required, or failing to supervise and administer safety of historic buildings and buildings with traditional character; or
(4) failing to perform other related duties, or failing to perform them as required, causing serious harm to protection of Zhenyuan, a historic and cultural city, or destructive effects on the historical, artistic, or cultural value of protected assets on the list.

articles.10:
Article 10 The protected matters of Fuzhou as a historic and cultural city mainly include:
(1) the traditional layout and historic character of the historic urban area;
(2) historic and cultural districts such as Sanfang Qixiang, Zhuzifang, and Shangxiahang;
(3) historic character areas such as Yantai Hill, Yeshan Hill, and the Fujian Shipbuilding Administration;
(4) groups of historic buildings such as Guling and the former site of Fujian Union University;
(5) historic and cultural towns and villages such as Luozhou and Min'an, and traditional villages;
(6) outstanding traditional streets and lanes;
(7) immovable cultural relics, historic buildings, and buildings of traditional character;
(8) intangible cultural heritage and other outstanding Mindu culture;
(9) historic environmental features such as ancient river and lake systems, hot springs, and ancient banyan trees and other ancient or notable trees; and
(10) other matters protected under laws and regulations.

Article 10 This Municipality shall maintain a protection list for the historic and cultural city. The following protected matters shall be included:
(1) historic and cultural districts;
(2) areas with historic character;
(3) famous historic and cultural towns and villages and traditional villages;
(4) immovable cultural relics, including protected cultural relics at all levels;
(5) historic buildings;
(6) modern industrial heritage;
(7) underground archaeological zones and ancient sites;
(8) historic environmental features such as ancient river and lake systems, ancient or notable trees, ancient bridges and roads, and ancient stone inscriptions;
(9) intangible cultural heritage such as local traditional literature, performing arts, folk customs, and traditional folk crafts;
(10) old place names with historic and cultural value or commemorative significance; and
(11) other protected matters designated and announced by the State, provincial, or Municipal People's Government.

articles.13:
Article 13 A building or structure that has existed for at least 50 years, has not been designated and announced as a protected cultural relic or registered as an immovable cultural relic, and meets any of the following criteria may be designated and announced as a historic building by the Municipal or County People's Government:
(1) association with an important historical event or figure;
(2) representativeness in the history of urban development or construction;
(3) representativeness in the development history of an industry;
(4) historic or cultural significance for commemoration or education;
(5) a distinctive design style representative of a particular period;
(6) architectural form with artistic characteristics or value;
(7) reflection of the architectural art of a particular locality or ethnic group;
(8) a landmark or symbol in a local urban or rural area and a source of shared community identity;
(9) a representative work of a renowned architect;
(10) building materials, structure, or construction techniques that reflect the engineering or scientific and technological level of the time;
(11) a building composition or spatial layout that was advanced for its period; or
(12) other distinctive value.
A building or structure that met the foregoing criteria but has been lost may be recognized as a historic building if it is reconstructed in its original form or relocated and reconstructed elsewhere.
